WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.068
NIST SP 800-82 Revision 3 provides practical guidance for operational technology security.

00:00:07.068 --> 00:00:19.896
It broadens the discussion beyond traditional industrial control systems while keeping physical process, safety, reliability, and availability at the center of security decisions.

00:00:19.896 --> 00:00:27.488
Effective programs understand their assets, topology, communication, dependencies, threats, vulnerabilities, and operational consequences.

00:00:27.488 --> 00:00:36.651
Risk management connects that evidence to accountable decisions about architecture, controls, remediation, residual risk, and recovery.

00:00:36.651 --> 00:00:47.385
Defense in depth combines physical protection, segmentation, access control, secure remote access, monitoring, vulnerability management, backups, and incident response.

00:00:47.385 --> 00:00:56.548
Architecture should prevent unnecessary direct access and provide controlled, observable paths between enterprise and operational functions.

00:00:56.548 --> 00:00:59.951
Security must also be maintained.

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:11.732
Inventory, configuration baselines, change control, patch planning, logging, training, exercises, and lifecycle management keep controls effective as technology and operations evolve.

00:01:11.732 --> 00:01:18.277
Evidence should demonstrate that controls work without creating unacceptable process risk.

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:24.298
The publication guides decisions but does not replace site-specific judgment.

00:01:24.298 --> 00:01:30.843
Cybersecurity professionals, engineers, operators, safety teams, vendors, and leadership must collaborate.

00:01:30.843 --> 00:01:49.954
The strongest outcome is not a completed checklist; it is a resilient operation that can prevent, detect, respond to, and recover from cyber events while continuing to protect people, mission, equipment, and the physical process.
